<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Regulamentul Organic</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1831">1831</a>–<a href="/years/1832">1832</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Organic Regulation, Organic Statute</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Regulamentul Organic was the quasi-constitutional law introduced in Wallachia in 1831 and Moldavia in 1832 while the principalities were under Russian occupation after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1828-1829">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1828–1829. Drafted under the Russian governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavel-kiselyov">Pavel Kiselyov</a>, it created modern ministries, budgets, a militia and assemblies dominated by the boyars, and remained the framework of government until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-convention-of-1858">Paris Convention of 1858</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/regulamentul-organic.jpg" alt="Regulamentul Organic" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wallachian_Regulamentul_organic_1832.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Moldavia and Wallachia had long paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, which ruled them through Phanariote princes appointed by the Porte, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> claimed a right to intervene on behalf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> subjects under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kucuk-kaynarca">Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca</a> of 1774. The Akkerman Convention of 7 October 1826 ended Phanariote rule, provided for princes elected for seven years by each <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divan">Divan</a>, granted free foreign trade and first mentioned new statutes to govern the two countries. These were drafted only after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1828-1829">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1828–1829, under Russian occupation.</p><p>The occupation was widely felt as abusive: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> seized both treasuries, and the governor Zheltukhin packed the drafting commission and expelled anti-Russian boyars such as Iancu Văcărescu. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavel-kiselyov">Pavel Kiselyov</a>, governor from 19 October 1829 to 1 April 1834, fought plague, cholera and famine and oversaw the enforcement of the new law.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Regulament partly confirmed the traditional government, including rule by the hospodars, set up a common Russian protectorate that lasted until 1854, and gave the two principalities their first common system of government. Towns received a local administration. In the countryside it favoured the great estates: peasants could keep about 70% of the harvest of a leased plot, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvee-labour">corvée</a> was fixed at up to 12 days a year, boyars could use a third of an estate as they pleased, and peasants were divided by wealth into fruntași, mijlocași and codași, the last with no right to pasture.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> also made each assembly pass an Additional Article forbidding any change without the consent of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>. In Wallachia this caused a four-year standstill, during which a nationalist group drafted its own constitution declaring the Russian protectorate and Ottoman suzerainty at an end. The Additional Article passed through the interference of Prince Alexandru II Ghica, and the group's radical leader, Ion Câmpineanu, had to take refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Though conservative, the statute opened a period of reform and the Westernization of local society. Trade grew, the urban population had doubled by 1850, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/braila">Brăila</a>, Giurgiu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galati">Galați</a> prospered on grain. Romanian Romantic nationalism flourished, with its interest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> origin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>. Under its last reforms Moldavia freed its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a> slaves on 22 December 1855 and Wallachia banned slave ownership on 20 February 1856. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1856">Treaty of Paris</a> of 30 March 1856 placed the principalities under the protection of all the European powers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Regulamentul Organic” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1831 the <b>Organic Statute</b> of 1832 repealed the constitution; voivodeships became governorates in 1837, the ruble became legal tender in 1841 and the Russian penal code replaced the Polish one.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1829 the <b>Organic Regulation</b> of 1832, introduced under Russian protection, created a modern administration and an assembly of boyars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Adrianople in 1829 and the <b>Regulamentul Organic</b> gave the two Danubian Principalities common institutions, a semblance of a constitution and a Legislative Assembly, which elected Gheorghe Bibescu in 1842.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-wallachia">Principality of Wallachia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Its constitution, the <b>Organic Statute</b>, was drafted by an international commission, and Plovdiv became its capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-rumelia">Eastern Rumelia</a></li></ul></section>
